# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Confidential / local data.
# Nothing under local-data/ is ever committed except its README.
# See docs/data-policy.md — anything that reaches the production bundle is public.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
local-data/*
!local-data/README.md

.env
.env.*

# Spreadsheets are blanket-ignored because a developer financial model is the
# single most likely confidential file to be dropped into this tree by accident.
# Keep it that way — the rule is a safety net, not a formatting preference.
*.xlsx
*.xlsm
*.xls
*.csv.local

# The one deliberate exception. tests/fixtures/workbooks/ holds shaped samples,
# a few kB each, committed on purpose so the ETL transforms are testable without
# a download — see the contract asserted in tests/etl-boundary.test.js, which
# caps them at 200 kB and fails if a real release is committed instead.
#
# The blanket rule above used to swallow them silently. They were never tracked,
# so a fresh clone had no fixtures and 38 tests failed in CI on every run while
# passing locally for anyone whose working tree still had the files. Do not
# remove this negation without deleting those tests.
!tests/fixtures/workbooks/*.xlsx

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ETL run artefacts.
# Drift reports and proposal bodies are per-run outputs, published as CI
# artefacts and job summaries rather than committed. The DATA the ETL writes
# (public/data/) is committed on purpose — that history is the audit trail.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
etl/out/

# Scratch: downloaded source PDFs, dev-server logs, anything a run left behind.
# Source documents are cited by URL in the source registers, not vendored.
tmp/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Project diligence outputs.
#
# deliverables/ is where the comparison workflow writes its gap reports and
# owner question lists. Those name real projects and quote real developer
# material, so nothing under it is committed — the default is deny.
#
# _comparison_system/ is the exception: it is the reusable METHOD (a skill
# definition, reference standards, a docx builder), carries no project facts,
# and is worth versioning. Adding a second exception here means asserting the
# same thing about it, so read the files first.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
deliverables/*
!deliverables/_comparison_system/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build + tooling
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
node_modules/
dist/
.vite/
*.local
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Editor
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json
.idea/
